g willing to infuse the  economy with liquidity if the economy  got into trouble this was a famous  Greenspan put and he said look we're  trying to help the economy the economy  gets into trouble we'll try and infuse  enough liquidity so as to make sure the  economy comes out of it again if there  was ever an excuse the financial sector  needed to go haywire to just go stretch  out on a limb and take risks  the Greenspan put was certainly one part  of it so what I want to argue then here  is that what we have is jobless  recoveries in the US overlaid on the  inequality and both these tend to  combine to create one directed credit  into housing to very stimulative  policies both of which tend to have an  effect on the financial sector so let me  come to the last part of what I want to  argue about what does all this have to  do with the crisis well we had a whole  lot of low quality mortgage-backed  securities created right why would this  creator  what role did the government play well  clearly the enormous amount of money  flooding into this sector from  government sources from foreign sources  money that didn't ask same kinds of  questions that the private sector would  have asked did play a role in making it  easier to sell these kinds of securities  and a lot of them were in fact sold so I  want to argue that's part of what was  what happened but the the critical  question we need to ask is why banks  held on to much of this risk and I want  to argue banks held on to much of this  risk because again the kinds of messages  that was being sent out by the monetary  and fiscal authorities was if you get  into trouble we will come to help you  and in fact that's what we've seen  happen and so the private financial  sector was in many ways incentivized to  do the kinds of things they did not  blameless they bear a big part of the  responsibility but it was part of the  incentive structure and let me end by  making those two points one why  mortgage-backed securities w