he other  side buying these securities had only  limited ways of checking what was in  them they focused more on the stuff that  they could check for example investors  in these mortgage-backed securities  could check the rating the credit rating  of the homeowners who held the  underlying mortgage so over time what  the bank's decided was I need a good  householder with an adequate credit  rating I don't care what else happens  all I want is a credit rating to be  right well once the mortgage broker  knows that all the bank is interested is  the credit rating of the homeowner he  works to massage the credit rating such  that that credit rating meets the bank's  norms and there was a lot of this  massaging going on so even while the  credit ratings of the house'll  homeowners who had borrowed remained  relatively high over time the underlying  quality was deteriorating why did all  this happen because at the end of the  line there is somebody willing to buy  the stuff without asking too many  questions there's a huge wall of money  pouring in and so to some extent the  wall of money does play a role in in in  determining  actually happen but you know the banks  who were issuing this stuff ended up  holding a whole lot of stuff on their  balance sheet while the banks hold this  stuff here didn't they know what was in  it and we have to we have to recognize  that you know it wasn't clear throughout  this process that we would have a crisis  of the order that we had what happened  was the incentives to maintain risk  controls to manage the risk were  weakened by the process that were going  on banks in many ways were taking a bet  and the bet that they were taking given  the nature of the financial system is  the best way to explain that is by  talking about earthquake insurance right  now in the financial sector in the  modern financial sector when you write  risks when you take on risks investors  are looking to see whether you make a  return over and above the risk 