m  do it how did it help them do it well  you know when the government says if  there's a liquidity problem I am gonna  help you out I'm going to come and flood  the market with the QWERTY then I have  an incentive to take the kinds of  investments which will suffer when  there's a collapse of liquidity because  at that point I know the government is  going to come in even better if  everybody's doing it because I know for  certain the government is going to come  in and and support the markets so at one  level what the banks were doing was  essentially rolling the dice taking big  liquidity players investing in  mortgage-backed securities which ninety  five ninety nine percent of the time  would work out well but with small  probability would be a disaster right  and they were financing it with huge  loads of debt again you could easily  roll over the debt most of the time when  times are good with small probability  financing would drive amp but you know  under those circumstances you would  toast now why do I care about those  circumstances when I'm toast especially  the government is going to bail me out  I'm going to take this risk I'm going to  take these risks in spades and there is  evidence that this is what was happening  when you look at the companies that the  the most risks these were the companies  that were rewarded with the highest  stock prices in the 1990s they were the  ones with had the highest stock returns  they were the ones that before the  crisis in 2006 had the highest stock  prices the market was rewarding this  kind of behavior because they knew that  on the upside the shareholders and  investors benefited on the downside the  gut the government took the hint and  exposed what we've seen is exactly this  happened right yes there were a few  unfortunate institutions Lehman went  down you know Bear Stearns barely got  anything but if you look at the crisis  being a small probability event it made  sense for all these entities to go out  on 