ession so let me given that we are  running out of time let me end with this  last point clearly in the financial  sector we need reforms the reforms to my  mind has have to focus not on equity not  on it has a focus on making sure that  investors absorb the downside risks that  they are creating that they're allowing  the banks to create and more than  anything else this means that debt  holders when there's a calamity of the  kind that we've seen bare some of the  costs of that calamity bear some of the  cost of the downside bear the hid and  therefore focusing on them absorbing the  head there has to be central to our  policies trying to get equity people say  it was a lack of governance what I'm  trying to argue that if you were  governing the bank in the interests of  your shareholders you would take these  beds because most of the time you would  take a much you make a ton of money in  fact that's what the stall Act while the  city banks of the world who took all  this risk their stock price zoomed the  market was cheering you on because these  kinds of risks made sense for the  shareholder so you can't rely on the  shareholder to control management to  keep these risks under control you have  to rely on the downside one important  source of downside would be if it hit  the debt holders  they would keep the bank from getting  too big for the US we need you the US to  have more effective policies especially  for downturns  I think structurally there is an issue  about the growing inequality and can we  resolve the growing inequality by  improving access to education for  example rather than focusing on  redistribution there's that debate is  alive in the u.s. today if we go down  the part of redistribution to my mind  that will remove some of the Flair and  innovation that exists in the US economy  we need to go down the path of improving  access to making sure more of the  citizenry get access to good sound  education how do we do that  how do we keep little people happy while  that's happening that's I think the  central question the United States has  to it has to face but I think the health  care debate sort of feeds into that  second as far as downturns go how do we  preserve the flexibility and the  innovation that is in the US economy as  a result of being able to hire and fire  relatively quickly even while  recognizing the nature of the recession  has changed and maybe the safety net  needs to be a stronger and more long-run  on safety net this to my mind are the  central political questions the u.s. has  to answer and we will answer them in  some fashion over the next few years but  if we don't answer them and we leave  these questions on tackled u.s. policy  is going to be relatively inadequate for  the needs of the moment and in u.s.  policy of course is a reaction to  another factor which is outside the US  is control which is the behavior of  countries outside the US the surplus  countries Japan Germany now China  increasingly joining that how do you  deal with these surplus countries and  get them to see that more bands growth  is in the world's interest that the u.s.  cannot continue to be the consumer of  last resort they cannot continue to rely  on bad US policy which results in the  kinds of downturns that we've seen the  kind of crisis that we've seen to hold  their growth up how do we get them to  play a bigger part in contributing to  world growth that to my mind is the  central question  facing global governance it's a question  that there are no easy answers to and  let me end at that point
 

